{"from": "observation:tomb-of-shroud-leprae-dna", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-02-leper-came", "grade": "C", "note": "The Tomb of the Shroud in Akeldama (first-century Jerusalem) yielded Mycobacterium leprae DNA - the earliest confirmed case of leprosy; the disease the leper suffered was present in Judea in the period."}
{"from": "site:akeldama-tombs", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-02-leper-came", "grade": "C", "note": "The Akeldama tombs include the first-century Tomb of the Shroud, 'one of very few examples of a preserved shrouded human burial' - real burials of real sufferers in the period; Akeldama is in Jerusalem, not Galilee."}
{"from": "observation:tomb-of-shroud-leprae-dna", "type": "attests", "to": "place:jerusalem", "grade": "C", "note": "First-century shrouded burial in the Hinnom Valley near Jerusalem yielded M. tuberculosis and M. leprae DNA - the city's 1st-century population and disease environment."}
{"from": "site:akeldama-tombs", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-28-demoniacs-tombs", "grade": "C", "note": "Rock-cut Second Temple period burial caves near Jerusalem: inhabited-region tombs were cut burial caves in the period (Akeldama is in Judea, not the Gadara region)."}
{"from": "site:gadara-ancient-tombs", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-28-demoniacs-tombs", "grade": "C", "note": "Early visitors to Umm Qais (Gadara) recorded 'the tombs and other monuments still visible on the surface' - tombs around the city of the demoniacs' country, though their date and form are not stated on the fetched page."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-04-offering-moses-commanded", "grade": "C", "note": "The Second Temple was the 'chief place of worship, ritual sacrifice (korban)' - the living institution to which a cleansed leper would be sent with the offering Moses commanded."}
{"from": "claim:mt-08-05-centurion-capernaum", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-matthew-8-5"}
{"from": "claim:mt-08-05-centurion-capernaum", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-matthew-8-5-gundry"}
{"from": "claim:mt-08-09-command-of-soldiers", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-matthew-8-5"}
{"from": "claim:mt-08-09-command-of-soldiers", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-matthew-8-5-gundry"}
{"from": "site:capernaum", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-05-centurion-capernaum", "grade": "B", "note": "Capernaum is excavated and was established in the 2nd century BC as a Hasmonean fishing village - a real lakeside town at the right place and time for the meeting."}
{"from": "site:capernaum-peter-house", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-14-mother-in-law-fever", "grade": "C", "note": "A first-century domestic dwelling under the octagonal church in Capernaum - ordinary family housing of the kind where a mother-in-law lay sick. Regraded B to C by review 2, matching the architect's regrade of the identical dwelling edge for the paralytic in ch 09 (context, not the specific house); the disputed identification as Peter's house is recorded separately as complicates."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-8-eleazar", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-16-evening-exorcisms", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus saw Eleazar 'releasing people that were demoniacal in the presence of Vespasian' - exorcism was a practiced craft among 1st-century Jews, the practice the crowds expected."}
{"from": "artifact:great-isaiah-scroll", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-17-isaiah-53-4-quoted", "grade": "B", "note": "The Great Isaiah Scroll (ca. 125 BCE) contains the entire Book of Isaiah including chapter 53 - the prophecy Matthew quotes existed in Hebrew over a century before. Graded B by architect decision: the scroll shows the quoted Isaiah text existed before Jesus, which is the specific point of a quotation claim."}
{"from": "observation:red-fox-range-levant", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-20-foxes-holes", "grade": "C", "note": "The red fox is the most widely distributed carnivore (entire Northern Hemisphere, incl. the Levant) and 'their burrows are often dug on hill or mountain slopes, in ravines' - foxes with holes are part of the region's fauna; no first-century Galilee-specific find was fetched."}
{"from": "artifact:second-temple-ossuaries", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-21-22-bury-their-dead", "grade": "C", "note": "In the Second Temple period primary burials in caves were followed by secondary burials in ossuaries - burying a father was a real multi-stage process that could delay a disciple for months."}
{"from": "artifact:galilee-boat", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-23-boat-crossing", "grade": "C", "note": "An ancient fishing boat from the 1st century AD, discovered on the north-west shore of the Sea of Galilee - boats of the kind used for crossings existed on the lake in the period (no evidence connects the boat to Jesus or his disciples). Regraded B to C by review 2, matching the architect's regrade of the boat for the return crossing in ch 09 (context, not the event)."}
{"from": "observation:sea-of-galilee-sudden-storms", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-24-sudden-storm", "grade": "C", "note": "Commentary records that the Sea of Galilee 'was known for its sudden and fierce storms', attributed by the Pulpit Commentary to winds arising on Mount Hermon - the storm the passage describes is a real, characteristic hazard (modern commentary, not ancient testimony)."}
{"from": "site:hippos", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-28-country-of-gergesenes", "grade": "C", "note": "Hippos, a Decapolis city on a ridge by the east shore of the lake - the 'other side' really was the Gentile Decapolis region; some modern scholars speculate Hippos itself as the location of the exorcism."}
{"from": "site:kursi", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-28-country-of-gergesenes", "grade": "D", "note": "Kursi's Byzantine monastery is traditionally identified as the location of the Miracle of the Swine, but the identification is disputed: Gergasa is not known before late antiquity and does not appear in early manuscripts."}
{"from": "site:kursi", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-30-herd-of-swine", "grade": "D", "note": "The same traditional identification makes Kursi the swine-miracle site; the monastery is Byzantine (excavated 1971-1974) and attests the commemorative tradition, not a first-century pig herd."}
