{"from": "text:leviticus-14", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-08-04-offering-moses-commanded", "note": "Leviticus 14 is the law of the cleansed leper: brought to the priest, examined, and offering two clean birds, cedar wood, scarlet and hyssop - 'the gift that Moses commanded'. Cross-reference only; it does not attest."}
{"from": "text:isaiah-53-4", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-08-17-isaiah-53-4-quoted", "note": "Isaiah 53:4 (WEB): 'Surely he has borne our sickness, and carried our suffering' - the text Matthew alludes to ('He took our infirmities, and bore our diseases'). Cross-reference only; it does not attest."}
{"from": "observation:tomb-of-shroud-leprae-dna", "type": "related_to", "to": "site:akeldama-tombs", "note": "The DNA observation comes from the Tomb of the Shroud within the Akeldama tombs site."}
{"from": "site:kursi", "type": "related_to", "to": "place:gadara", "note": "Kursi and Gadara (Umm Qais) are the two rival candidate sites for the Gadarene/Gergesene country of Matthew 8:28."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-113-115", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikisource-ant18-aretas"}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-113-115", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-05-centurion-capernaum", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus (Ant. 18.113-115) records that in the war with Aretas IV 'they raised armies on both sides' and 'all Herod's army was destroyed' - ancient evidence that Antipas maintained his own army, the military milieu of a centurion in his realm. Josephus names no centurion and no Capernaum garrison, so this is context only (lead attached by review 2)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-113-115", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-08-09-command-of-soldiers", "grade": "C", "note": "The same passage: Antipas commanded 'armies' with 'generals' against Aretas IV - a tetrarch's standing chain of command, the world of the centurion's 'I say to one Go and he goes'. The passage names no centurion, so it evidences the setting, not the officer (lead attached by review 2)."}
{"from": "site:capernaum-peter-house", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:mt-08-14-mother-in-law-fever", "note": "The excavators identified the house as Peter's, venerated from the mid-1st century, but other scholars question the identification (very little legible graffiti; whether the space is a room is disputed) - the claim's 'Peter's house' is not archaeologically secured (review 2 retargeted the former site-to-person attestation to this claim as its tension)."}
